% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Justin Jefferson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to CHI \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>CHI \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CHI are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 4-point favorite projected to lead for 33% of snaps, <strong>CHI</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>12 carries for 53 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as CHI salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Rome Odunze</strong> (6 targets, 43 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when CHI lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Colston Loveland</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 60 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>MIN \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>MIN are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage i